1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,625
A download is ready to send millions of bytes.
Somewhere ahead

2
00:00:04,625 --> 00:00:09,250
is a connection that limits how quickly they
can pass, but

3
00:00:09,250 --> 00:00:13,875
the sender does not begin with a map of its
available

4
00:00:13,875 --> 00:00:18,500
capacity. Sending everything immediately could
overwhelm it. Sending painfully little forever

5
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:23,125
wastes an opportunity. So how does a sender
start? Watch the

6
00:00:23,125 --> 00:00:27,750
first packets and their returning
acknowledgments. They will do more than

7
00:00:27,750 --> 00:00:32,375
confirm delivery: they will help pace a
growing experiment called TCP

8
00:00:32,375 --> 00:00:37,000
slow start.

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,679
Our picture has a sender, a receiver, and a
bottleneck along

10
00:00:41,679 --> 00:00:46,358
the path. Assume the receiver has plenty of
free buffer space,

11
00:00:46,358 --> 00:00:51,038
so its advertised window is not the limit in
this story.

12
00:00:51,038 --> 00:00:55,717
We are focusing on the sender's congestion
window, usually called cwnd.

13
00:00:55,717 --> 00:01:00,396
It bounds how many bytes may be outstanding
without acknowledgment. That

14
00:01:00,396 --> 00:01:05,075
is a quantity of data, not a transmission
speed printed on

15
00:01:05,075 --> 00:01:09,754
the cable. Keeping those two meanings separate
will make the later

16
00:01:09,754 --> 00:01:14,433
equation much easier.

17
00:01:14,433 --> 00:01:19,000
For our illustration, begin with room for four
full-size segments. Four

18
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:23,567
is a teaching choice, not a claim that every
TCP connection

19
00:01:23,567 --> 00:01:28,133
starts there. Initial-window choices have
changed across specifications and
implementations. We

20
00:01:28,133 --> 00:01:32,700
are also assuming one useful acknowledgment
for each new segment and

21
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:37,267
steady round-trip timing. These clean
assumptions let us see the mechanism

22
00:01:37,267 --> 00:01:41,833
before real network variation blurs it. The
sender has more data

23
00:01:41,833 --> 00:01:46,400
ready, but only these first four segments are
allowed into the

24
00:01:46,400 --> 00:01:50,967
experiment.

25
00:01:50,967 --> 00:01:55,533
One, two, three, four. The sender puts those
segments onto the

26
00:01:55,533 --> 00:02:00,100
path, then waits for progress to be confirmed.
At this point,

27
00:02:00,100 --> 00:02:04,667
there can be four segments of unacknowledged
data in flight. They

28
00:02:04,667 --> 00:02:09,233
are not travelling on four new cables. They
are sharing the

29
00:02:09,233 --> 00:02:13,800
same path at different positions. If the
sender released its whole

30
00:02:13,800 --> 00:02:18,367
file just because the first segment had left
its machine, it

31
00:02:18,367 --> 00:02:22,933
would be ignoring the very uncertainty slow
start is trying to

32
00:02:22,933 --> 00:02:27,500
manage.

33
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:32,233
The first acknowledgment comes back. Part of
its effect is straightforward:

34
00:02:32,233 --> 00:02:36,967
the confirmed data no longer counts as
outstanding. There is now

35
00:02:36,967 --> 00:02:41,700
room to send a replacement segment within the
old window. But

36
00:02:41,700 --> 00:02:46,433
slow start also increases the congestion
window when new data is

37
00:02:46,433 --> 00:02:51,167
acknowledged, subject to its rules. In our
simplified full-segment example, it

38
00:02:51,167 --> 00:02:55,900
adds one more segment of allowance. One
acknowledgment therefore frees old

39
00:02:55,900 --> 00:03:00,633
space and helps create new space. That
combination is what makes

40
00:03:00,633 --> 00:03:05,367
the next round grow.

41
00:03:05,367 --> 00:03:10,100
Repeat that process for the four initial
segments. Each acknowledgment confirms

42
00:03:10,100 --> 00:03:14,833
progress and contributes one segment to our
illustrative window growth. By

43
00:03:14,833 --> 00:03:19,567
the end of this round, cwnd has increased from
four segments

44
00:03:19,567 --> 00:03:24,300
to eight. The sender can now have twice as
much unacknowledged

45
00:03:24,300 --> 00:03:29,033
data outstanding. It did not ask a router for
an exact

46
00:03:29,033 --> 00:03:33,767
bandwidth number. It used successful delivery
feedback to expand cautiously from

47
00:03:33,767 --> 00:03:38,500
its starting point. The growth is emerging
from the acknowledgment rule,

48
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:43,233
not a stopwatch alone.

49
00:03:43,233 --> 00:03:48,200
Look closely at the returning arrows.
Acknowledgments do not just arrive

50
00:03:48,200 --> 00:03:53,167
after an abstract round has finished; each one
can permit new

51
00:03:53,167 --> 00:03:58,133
sending as it arrives. That is an ACK clock.
The flow

52
00:03:58,133 --> 00:04:03,100
of feedback helps pace the outgoing flow of
data. Our round-by-round

53
00:04:03,100 --> 00:04:08,067
grouping makes the count easier to follow, but
a real sender

54
00:04:08,067 --> 00:04:13,033
does not have to wait for all acknowledgments
in a batch

55
00:04:13,033 --> 00:04:18,000
before doing anything. It reacts as progress
arrives.

56
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:22,567
After eight segments are acknowledged under
our assumptions, the window grows

57
00:04:22,567 --> 00:04:27,133
by another eight, reaching sixteen. Then
sixteen successful segment acknowledgments can

58
00:04:27,133 --> 00:04:31,700
take it to thirty-two. Why doubling? Start
with W segments of

59
00:04:31,700 --> 00:04:36,267
allowance, add roughly W more over a round
trip, and the

60
00:04:36,267 --> 00:04:40,833
next round has about two W. The exponential-
looking graph is the

61
00:04:40,833 --> 00:04:45,400
accumulated result of many small increases.
The name slow start sounds

62
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:49,967
modest, but its early growth can become
surprisingly quick across several

63
00:04:49,967 --> 00:04:54,533
rounds.

64
00:04:54,533 --> 00:04:59,267
We can now write the pattern instead of
memorizing it. Starting

65
00:04:59,267 --> 00:05:04,000
from four, the idealized window after k
complete growth rounds is

66
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:08,733
four times two to the power k. Zero rounds
gives four;

67
00:05:08,733 --> 00:05:13,467
one gives eight; two gives sixteen; three
gives thirty-two. The equation

68
00:05:13,467 --> 00:05:18,200
describes the clean picture we just built.
Delayed acknowledgments, different growth

69
00:05:18,200 --> 00:05:22,933
rules, losses, and application limits can
change the actual trace. So

70
00:05:22,933 --> 00:05:27,667
use this model to understand the mechanism,
rather than as a

71
00:05:27,667 --> 00:05:32,400
prediction for every download.

72
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:37,367
Suppose each segment carries one thousand
bytes, to keep the arithmetic

73
00:05:37,367 --> 00:05:42,333
visible. A sixteen-segment window holds
sixteen thousand bytes of outstanding data.

74
00:05:42,333 --> 00:05:47,300
If that much data can be acknowledged every
hundred milliseconds, the

75
00:05:47,300 --> 00:05:52,267
window supports an average of one hundred
sixty thousand bytes per

76
00:05:52,267 --> 00:05:57,233
second. Multiply by eight and that is about
one point two

77
00:05:57,233 --> 00:06:02,200
eight megabits per second. This is a window-
limited approximation, not a

78
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:07,167
promise that the path can deliver that rate.

79
00:06:07,167 --> 00:06:12,195
Keep the same sixteen thousand bytes, but
double the round-trip time

80
00:06:12,195 --> 00:06:17,224
to two hundred milliseconds. The approximate
rate halves, because replenishing the

81
00:06:17,224 --> 00:06:22,252
same allowance takes twice as long. Now the
expression window divided

82
00:06:22,252 --> 00:06:27,281
by round-trip time has a visible meaning: an
amount of outstanding

83
00:06:27,281 --> 00:06:32,310
data, renewed over a period of time. A larger
window can

84
00:06:32,310 --> 00:06:37,338
help keep a longer path busy, but it cannot
force more

85
00:06:37,338 --> 00:06:42,367
through a bottleneck than that connection can
actually transmit.

86
00:06:42,367 --> 00:06:46,992
Our growing train eventually reaches a point
where the bottleneck cannot

87
00:06:46,992 --> 00:06:51,617
serve arrivals as quickly as they build up.
Packets wait, and

88
00:06:51,617 --> 00:06:56,242
the feedback reaches the sender later.
Congestion may also lead to

89
00:06:56,242 --> 00:07:00,867
packet loss or explicit signals. Successful
earlier rounds did not prove

90
00:07:00,867 --> 00:07:05,492
that unlimited growth would remain safe. They
only justified increasing from

91
00:07:05,492 --> 00:07:10,117
the earlier operating point. Slow start
explores available capacity through feedback;

92
00:07:10,117 --> 00:07:14,742
it does not receive advance permission to
overfill every buffer on

93
00:07:14,742 --> 00:07:19,367
the path.

94
00:07:19,367 --> 00:07:24,395
TCP has a slow-start threshold, commonly
called ssthresh. While the congestion

95
00:07:24,395 --> 00:07:29,424
window is below that threshold, slow-start
growth can continue. Reaching the

96
00:07:29,424 --> 00:07:34,452
threshold or observing congestion changes what
happens next. We will stop

97
00:07:34,452 --> 00:07:39,481
our explanation at that boundary, because slow
start is one part

98
00:07:39,481 --> 00:07:44,510
of a larger congestion-control process. The
point is that doubling is

99
00:07:44,510 --> 00:07:49,538
not meant to continue forever. The same graph
that explains its

100
00:07:49,538 --> 00:07:54,567
speed also explains why it needs a stopping
condition.

101
00:07:54,567 --> 00:07:59,662
Try starting with eight segments instead of
four in the same

102
00:07:59,662 --> 00:08:04,757
idealized example. The growth sequence becomes
eight, sixteen, thirty-two. That reaches

103
00:08:04,757 --> 00:08:09,852
the thirty-two-segment level one round
earlier. A larger initial window can

104
00:08:09,852 --> 00:08:14,948
save an early round, but it also begins by
placing more

105
00:08:14,948 --> 00:08:20,043
unconfirmed data on an unknown path. This is a
trade-off, not

106
00:08:20,043 --> 00:08:25,138
free speed. Standards and implementations make
practical initial-window choices; our
animation

107
00:08:25,138 --> 00:08:30,233
isolates how that starting choice changes the
first few rounds.

108
00:08:30,233 --> 00:08:34,800
Before turning this tidy curve into a
universal rule, check the

109
00:08:34,800 --> 00:08:39,367
assumptions. Does the sender have enough data
ready? Does the receiver

110
00:08:39,367 --> 00:08:43,933
have room? Are acknowledgments delayed or
grouped? Is another bottleneck already

111
00:08:43,933 --> 00:08:48,500
limiting the flow? Each can alter what you
observe. The congestion

112
00:08:48,500 --> 00:08:53,067
window is permission to have data outstanding,
rather than an order

113
00:08:53,067 --> 00:08:57,633
to send at a smooth rate. The useful
explanation survives those

114
00:08:57,633 --> 00:09:02,200
complications: confirmed progress helps the
sender grow and pace its early

115
00:09:02,200 --> 00:09:06,767
traffic.

116
00:09:06,767 --> 00:09:11,558
So the sender did not discover a perfect speed
setting written

117
00:09:11,558 --> 00:09:16,350
somewhere inside the network. It began with
limited outstanding data, used

118
00:09:16,350 --> 00:09:21,142
acknowledgments to make room and grow that
allowance, and stopped the

119
00:09:21,142 --> 00:09:25,933
rapid growth when its rules required a
transition. The math, the

120
00:09:25,933 --> 00:09:30,725
window, and the moving packets were three
views of that same

121
00:09:30,725 --> 00:09:35,517
feedback process. Slow start is how a
connection can begin exploring

122
00:09:35,517 --> 00:09:40,308
an uncertain path without treating either
permanent caution or an immediate

123
00:09:40,308 --> 00:09:45,100
flood as its only option.
